**My forecast: 5% YES**

Trump has publicly entertained the possibility of demolishing the Kennedy Centre, I believe there are still very significant obstacles to this happening before the end of 2026.&#x20;

The federal court's requirement is for 30 days' notice before demolition, alongside the existing renovation plans and potential legal challenges, makes the timeframe quite restrictive.&#x20;

I think that there's an important distinction between Trump's public rhetoric and the actual steps necessary to make the demolition happen.&#x20;

So I have settled on 5% as I think that the timeframe and legal/logistical barriers outweigh the possibility of Trump following through before the deadline.&#x20;

I think the crowd is too low on this. The [*Washington Post*](https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2026/10/01/trump-does-not-rule-out-demolishing-kennedy-center/) reports that Trump was asked about the Kennedy Center in a Time interview and said: “I’m not committing to anything.” He added that they would “see how it plays out in court.” He again described the building as being in very poor physical condition and said he would be willing to save it, while continuing to connect the renovation dispute to whether he receives recognition.

Judge Christopher Cooper ordered the administration to provide [at least 30 days' written notice](https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/court-filing-cites-trump-comments-photo-reading-kennedy-center-demolished-2026-09-17/) before changing the scope of the project, explicitly including demolition of the main building. This means a lawful demolition cannot simply begin overnight in the way contemplated by some of the East Wing analogies. The notice period would give the plaintiffs and court time to respond, and [Axios](https://www.axios.com/local/washington-dc/2026/09/21/kennedy-center-demolition-trump) reported legal experts saying an attempt to proceed anyway could expose participants to contempt proceedings.&#x20;

The Kennedy Center extended its [emergency closure](https://www.washingtonpost.com/style/2026/09/23/kennedy-center-extends-closure-week-reopening-date-remains-uncertain/) for another week. Executive Director Matt Floca cited studies of roof damage that he said posed risks to pedestrians and indicated that the closure would continue to be reevaluated weekly. That matters because the **condition of the building is increasingly central to the dispute**. The more evidence officials produce that repair is impractical or unsafe, the more plausible replacement rather than renovation could become. [AP](https://apnews.com/article/kennedy-center-repairs-closing-trump-whistleblowers-283f2ac3a3b44223751687ed8569fd52) reported allegations from former Kennedy Center employees that officials had known about some structural problems earlier and that Floca had halted a funded roof-repair plan in April. &#x20;

This creates two competing narratives:

* **Administration/Center:** deterioration is sufficiently serious to justify closure and major intervention.
* **Former employees/critics:** some of the problems were already known and could have been addressed through repairs.

Also, Sen. Jeff Merkley and Sen. Lisa Murkowski introduced a [bipartisan proposal](https://www.merkley.senate.gov/merkley-pingree-lead-bipartisan-bill-to-prevent-demolition-of-the-kennedy-center/) that would require an act of Congress before a congressionally designated presidential memorial such as the Kennedy Center could be demolished. Murkowski said Congress had authorized substantial renovation work but had not authorized demolition. However, Sen. Mike Lee objected when Merkley tried to pass it by unanimous consent. So the proposal did not immediately become law and would need to move through the normal legislative process. That's somewhat mixed evidence for the question? Congressional opposition to demolition is now concrete and bipartisan.

I think the important variable is whether engineering reports ultimately make demolition/reconstruction an official project recommendation. The single most important future indicator is: watch for a court filing giving the required 30-day notice of a change in project scope involving demolition.&#x20;

